   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">It sounds like you&#39;re looking for open population models, which are implemented in unmarked with the <a rel=\"nofollow\">pcountopen function</a>. <div>Some papers to get you started: </div><div>- <a href=\"https://sci-hub.tw/https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1541-0420.2010.01465.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://sci-hub.tw/https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1541-0420.2010.01465.x&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792450523277000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3TVVg9VktJrfL928UhPZil\">Dail &amp; Madsen 2011</a></div><div>- <a href=\"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1890/14-1487.1?casa_token=nmFbNqRiO98AAAAA:O3Z2v4P2lGHPOdZY7JASwJ7E5Go4vpc_yTJr2LHIlnX2ru5YKJ88azWfSJqxVE6Qd2UxIwt1SLp3w84\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1890/14-1487.1?casa_token%3DnmFbNqRiO98AAAAA:O3Z2v4P2lGHPOdZY7JASwJ7E5Go4vpc_yTJr2LHIlnX2ru5YKJ88azWfSJqxVE6Qd2UxIwt1SLp3w84&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792450523277000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3CKVxiue3Rz9IDmc4dcneZ\">Hostetler and Chandler 2015</a></div><div>- <a href=\"https://sci-hub.tw/https://doi.org/10.1111/2041-210X.12572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://sci-hub.tw/https://doi.org/10.1111/2041-210X.12572&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792450523277000&amp;usg=AOvVaw045sZRA6KGYeE2vKGP3cd4\">Bellier, Kéry, and Schaub 2016</a></div><div>- <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10144-017-0600-7.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10144-017-0600-7.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792450523277000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2l6bI9jVV7kiaSQDWSSqHh\">Zhao, Royle, and Boomer 2017</a></div><div>- and a reminder that these models can be pretty sensitive to model assumptions: <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304380018300607/pdfft?casa_token=d8foSARhaLwAAAAA:lEGlCqaHYuwrfeMWDQ-C9sfbRfhU7J3TC3UpxY4lIFE5VFaUi6F0eQOCWCVQir0GVtxNLB2jRQ&amp;md5=01f6f53db8c905a4653bc5a5797f9b08&amp;pid=1-s2.0-S0304380018300607-main.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304380018300607/pdfft?casa_token%3Dd8foSARhaLwAAAAA:lEGlCqaHYuwrfeMWDQ-C9sfbRfhU7J3TC3UpxY4lIFE5VFaUi6F0eQOCWCVQir0GVtxNLB2jRQ%26md5%3D01f6f53db8c905a4653bc5a5797f9b08%26pid%3D1-s2.0-S0304380018300607-main.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792450523277000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1-6Xzh8HtkPPxFrw87SumS\">Duarte, Adams, and Peterson 2018</a> , <a href=\"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1002/ecs2.2791\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1002/ecs2.2791&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792450523277000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1eZpFFwA9c5E1sQA-johrJ\">Monroe et al. 2019</a> or <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304380018304186/pdfft?casa_token=7MncqkF94_MAAAAA:GgCrHgEVbuH0SZ3rYnMp1N63PtX0pVlsJhhye_QoCAUJ6KtpoW5nxjo1APkhFUKvOr6ry-ed4g&amp;md5=a2d5c398759ef4050b9c049c75e98335&amp;pid=1-s2.0-S0304380018304186-main.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304380018304186/pdfft?casa_token%3D7MncqkF94_MAAAAA:GgCrHgEVbuH0SZ3rYnMp1N63PtX0pVlsJhhye_QoCAUJ6KtpoW5nxjo1APkhFUKvOr6ry-ed4g%26md5%3Da2d5c398759ef4050b9c049c75e98335%26pid%3D1-s2.0-S0304380018304186-main.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792450523277000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0le5hAOWsADSZaByXGHGpx\">Zhao and Royle 2019</a></div><div>- The soon(?) to be released <a href=\"https://www.elsevier.com/books/applied-hierarchical-modeling-in-ecology-analysis-of-distribution-abundance-and-species-richness-in-r-and-bugs/kery/978-0-12-809585-0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.elsevier.com/books/applied-hierarchical-modeling-in-ecology-analysis-of-distribution-abundance-and-species-richness-in-r-and-bugs/kery/978-0-12-809585-0&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792450523277000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0VL_gnDq4Yd9ksPbsNdYVk\">volume 2</a> of Applied Hierarchical Modeling in Ecology</div><div><div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"color:rgb(136,136,136);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:medium\">Philip Shirk</span><br><div><div style=\"color:rgb(136,136,136);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:medium\">PhD, Zoology<br>University of Florida</div><div style=\"color:rgb(136,136,136);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:medium\">P.O. Box 118525<br>Gainesville FL, 32611-8525</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><br></div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Fri, May 8, 2020 at 1:34 PM Justin T Mann &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jma...@binghamton.edu</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello everyone, <div>I&#39;m very new to unmarked, and I&#39;m struggling to learn the ins and outs of this package during this pandemic when I have four children at home! I bet that my question has been addressed in other posts, but I&#39;m having trouble finding answers in the very limited time I have to work. If anyone could offer advice or point me in the right direction, I would greatly appreciate it. </div><div><br></div><div>I conducted an experiment using 37 survey locations across three study areas. One year prior to experimental treatments, I conducted 2 point count surveys at all survey locations to estimate baseline occupancy/abundance prior to experimental treatments. During experimental seasons, I conducted 5 point count surveys. I would like to model changes in abundance/occupancy with treatment (experimental or control), vegetation characteristics, and study area as covariates.     </div><div><br></div><div>My question is:</div><div><br></div><div>How can I model changes in abundance/occupancy during experimental seasons while controlling for the baseline abundance/occupancy based on my pre-treatment baseline surveys? </div><div><br></div><div>Thanks in advance,</div><div>Justin</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;unmarked&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">unmarked+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/unmarked/9caeacce-24da-4e71-8015-d1537cef3d71%40googlegroups.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/unmarked/9caeacce-24da-4e71-8015-d1537cef3d71%2540googlegroups.com?utm_medium%3Demail%26utm_source%3Dfooter&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792450523277000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1MYlOlibarrDP7Aj8_qUYD\">https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/unmarked/9caeacce-24da-4e71-8015-d1537cef3d71%40googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\n</blockquote></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
